# Thermus environment settings
#
# This file shows each setting with a placeholder value.
# Set these values in the environment of the server process.
# Do not write a real secret in this file.
# Startup checks the settings. A missing or incorrect setting stops startup.
# Unless a setting below states otherwise, an empty value is the same as a missing value.
#
# The container image (Dockerfile) sets DEPLOYMENT=self-hosted,
# DATA_DIR=/var/lib/thermus, AD_RULES_FILE=/etc/thermus/ad-rules.txt,
# LISTEN_HOST=0.0.0.0, LISTEN_PORT=8080, and NODE_ENV=production.
# compose.yaml sets PUBLIC_BASE_URL, by default http://localhost:8080, mounts one
# volume at DATA_DIR, and mounts ./ad-rules.txt read-only at AD_RULES_FILE.
# README.md#container-installation gives the commands.

# DEPLOYMENT selects the edition. No other setting selects the edition.
# Use "self-hosted" or "hosted". Write the value exactly: lowercase, with no spaces.
# There is no AUTH_MODE, no mock mode, and no setting that enables paid features.
# A change of DEPLOYMENT does not move accounts or data to the other edition.
DEPLOYMENT=self-hosted

# PUBLIC_BASE_URL is the public address of the server. Both editions use it.
# Use an absolute URL. Do not include a username, password, query, or fragment.
# The MCP address is this URL plus "mcp".
# For example, https://thermus.example gives https://thermus.example/mcp.
# The hosted edition must use https with the root base path /.
# The self-hosted edition must use https. For local development only, it can use
# http on localhost, 127.0.0.1, or [::1]. For example: http://localhost:8080
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://thermus.example

# Optional listen settings. Both editions read them.
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL does not set the listen address. The server can listen on
# another host or port, for example behind a reverse proxy or in a container.

# LISTEN_HOST is the address that the HTTP server listens on.
# The default is 127.0.0.1, which accepts connections from this machine only.
# Use 0.0.0.0 in a container, so that a published port can reach the server.
# LISTEN_HOST=127.0.0.1

# LISTEN_PORT is the TCP port of the HTTP server: a whole number from 0 to 65535.
# The default is 8080. With 0, the system selects a free port, and the server
# writes the address that it uses to its log.
# LISTEN_PORT=8080

# DATA_DIR is a self-hosted setting. Both server editions read AD_RULES_FILE.

# DATA_DIR is the absolute path of the data directory. The directory must exist.
# Put this directory on persistent storage, for example a container volume.
# The first start writes these files into it:
#   auth.sqlite          the authentication database and the installation identity
#   auth.secret          the generated secret of the provider; keep it secret
#   data-directory.lock  the lock that lets only one Thermus process use the data
# The first "clients register" command writes one more file; keep it secret:
#   resource-server-client.json  the client that the MCP server uses to check tokens
# Back up and restore the complete directory while the server is stopped.
# The database and auth.secret must always come from the same backup.
# A change of a setting is not a migration: no setting moves accounts or data.
DATA_DIR=/var/lib/thermus

# AD_RULES_FILE is the absolute path of the trusted ad rules file. The server
# requires it. The local account and client commands do not read it.
# Write Adblock Plus rules as UTF-8 text. The server reads the file one time when
# it starts, so a change takes effect at the next start. There is no size limit
# and no syntax check: the parser ignores the lines that it cannot parse.
# An empty file, or a file with only comments, gives no ad rules.
# A missing or relative value, a file that the server cannot read, or a file
# that is not UTF-8 stops startup before listening or opening local DATA_DIR.
# The startup error does not show the path or the file contents.
# The container image uses this path. compose.yaml mounts ./ad-rules.txt here.
AD_RULES_FILE=/etc/thermus/ad-rules.txt

# Better Auth reads some variables from the process environment, also when
# Thermus sets the related setting. Do not set these variables. Startup stops
# when one of them has a value, also BETTER_AUTH_TELEMETRY=0:
#   BETTER_AUTH_SECRETS can replace the secret in DATA_DIR/auth.secret.
#   BETTER_AUTH_TRUSTED_ORIGINS can add trusted origins to the origin, CSRF,
#   and callback URL checks.
#   BETTER_AUTH_TELEMETRY can turn on telemetry.
# NODE_ENV and TEST do not turn off the origin and CSRF checks.

# Hosted settings. Only DEPLOYMENT=hosted reads them.
# The hosted edition uses WorkOS AuthKit for browser sign-in and WorkOS Connect
# for MCP clients. Its WorkOS provider reads the five required settings below
# and checks them before the server listens.
# A missing or refused setting stops startup: there is no fallback to local or anonymous access. The error names
# each setting and its rule, but never a value. The hosted edition reads no
# data directory. It serves MCP over HTTPS only. Hosted startup rejects stdio.
# docs/design/authentication-design.md#hosted-browser-sessions tells how to
# configure the WorkOS environment.

# WORKOS_API_KEY is the secret API key of the WorkOS environment. It starts with
# sk_. Keep it secret: put it only in the environment of the server process.
# WORKOS_API_KEY=sk_example

# WORKOS_CLIENT_ID is the client ID of the WorkOS environment. It starts with
# client_. It is also the identity authority of the hosted users.
# WORKOS_CLIENT_ID=client_example

# WORKOS_AUTHKIT_ISSUER is the https origin of the AuthKit domain, exactly as
# WorkOS shows it, without a path or a final slash. Connect access tokens must
# carry this issuer.
# WORKOS_AUTHKIT_ISSUER=https://example.authkit.app

# WORKOS_REDIRECT_URI is the sign-in callback of the server: an https URL on the
# origin of PUBLIC_BASE_URL, inside its path. Register it as the redirect URI of
# the WorkOS environment. For example: https://thermus.example/callback
# WORKOS_REDIRECT_URI=https://thermus.example/callback

# WORKOS_COOKIE_PASSWORD seals the session cookies. Use at least 32 random
# characters, and keep it secret. A new value ends each open browser session.
# WORKOS_COOKIE_PASSWORD=replace-with-at-least-32-random-characters

# SELF_SERVE_SIGNUP_ENABLED accepts exactly true or false; omitted means false.
# It controls new hosted activation, not existing access. A change needs a restart.
# Keep it false: production has no approved signup document bundle yet, so true
# stops startup. FS-01 adds the guarded flow; later release work supplies approved
# documents and authorizes opening. No policy draft is shipped by this setting.
# SELF_SERVE_SIGNUP_ENABLED=false

# Renderer setting. Both server editions read it.

# LIGHTPANDA_BIN is the absolute path of the Lightpanda executable.
# The server reads it when it starts and gives it to the fetcher.
# The value is a path only. Do not add flags or quote characters.
# Hosted startup requires an absolute path and runs a bounded version check
# for Lightpanda 1.0.0 before listening. It stops if that check fails.
# Self-hosted startup does not check the renderer. If the setting is empty or
# not set, its fetcher runs lightpanda from PATH. A missing executable then
# makes each page fetch fail with "Page fetch failed".
# The container image holds the pinned renderer and sets LIGHTPANDA_BIN to it.
# docs/lightpanda-runtime.md tells how to install and verify Lightpanda 1.0.0.
# LIGHTPANDA_BIN=/absolute/path/to/lightpanda

# Hosted capacity setting. Only DEPLOYMENT=hosted reads it.

# OPERATION_CAPACITY is the number of fetch or search calls that run at the
# same time in one hosted process. Fetch and search share this capacity.
# Use a whole number from 1 to 100. The default is 10, also for an empty value.
# When the capacity is full, the next call gets "Server busy. Try again later."
# and the completion status busy. There is no queue. The capacity is for one
# process only: each instance has its own capacity. Each running call can start
# one renderer process, so a larger value needs more CPU and memory.
# An invalid value stops startup before listening. The error names the setting
# and the rule, but not the value. The server reads the setting when it starts,
# so a change needs a restart. The self-hosted edition has no capacity limit.
# A tenant (THERMUS_ROLE=tenant, below) reads the same setting. The provisioner
# sets 5 for a free tenant on 0.5c-512mb and 10 for a team tenant on 1c-2g.
# OPERATION_CAPACITY=10

# Hosted MCP gateway of the control plane (SO-05). Only DEPLOYMENT=hosted reads
# MCP_TENANTS_ENABLED, and the settings below it only when it is true.

# MCP_TENANTS_ENABLED is the rollout switch of the tenant containers: true or
# false, written exactly. The default is false, also for an empty value; another
# value stops startup with an error that names the setting. With false, the
# control plane reads none of the settings below and runs the tools itself, as
# before. With true, MCPServer forwards each MCP request that passes the bearer
# check and the access check to the tenant of its organization, with a signed
# access context, and /internal/audit takes the audit relays of the tenants.
# The switch needs the private hosted storage below: it holds the tenant
# registry. docs/design/authentication-design.md#mcp-gateway-and-audit-relay
# gives the states, the answers, and the relay rules.
# MCP_TENANTS_ENABLED=false

# GATEWAY_SIGNING_KEY is the Ed25519 private key of the gateway, in PEM form
# (-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----, PKCS #8). It signs the access context of each
# forwarded request. Only the control plane holds it; each tenant gets its public
# key in GATEWAY_PUBLIC_KEY. Keep it secret. Make a key pair with
# openssl genpkey -algorithm ed25519 and openssl pkey -pubout.
# GATEWAY_SIGNING_KEY=-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----...-----END PRIVATE KEY-----

# CONTROL_PLANE_INTERNAL_URL of the control plane is its origin on the private
# network, the value that each tenant gets (see the tenant settings below). The
# route /internal/audit answers only requests whose Host header is the host of
# this origin; each other request to /internal/* gets 404.
# CONTROL_PLANE_INTERNAL_URL=http://thermus-control-plane:8080

# FREE_TENANT_LIMIT is the number of free tenants that can hold a place in the
# tenant registry at the same time. The control plane reads it only with
# MCP_TENANTS_ENABLED=true; without the switch, the cap is 25. Each row holds a
# place, except a failed or a deleted one. Use a whole number from 0 to 1000.
# The default is 25, also for an empty value. 0 closes free signup through the
# cap. At a full cap, a tenant request for a new organization inserts nothing.
# An invalid value stops startup before listening. The error names the setting
# and the rule, but not the value. The server reads the setting when it starts,
# so a change needs a restart.
# FREE_TENANT_LIMIT=25

# Hosted role settings. Only DEPLOYMENT=hosted reads them.

# THERMUS_ROLE selects the role of the hosted image: control-plane, the
# default, or tenant. Write the value exactly. An empty value is control-plane;
# another value stops startup with an error that names the setting.
# The control plane is the hosted edition above, with the WorkOS settings.
# A tenant serves only /healthz and /mcp for one organization, behind the MCP
# gateway of the control plane. It holds no secret: each MCP request needs a
# signed access context of the control plane (the Thermus-Access header) and
# a Connect token with a valid AuthKit signature. Besides DEPLOYMENT,
# THERMUS_ROLE, LISTEN_HOST, and LISTEN_PORT, a tenant reads only
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL (the public value, with the root base path /), the four
# settings below, AD_RULES_FILE, LIGHTPANDA_BIN, and OPERATION_CAPACITY.
# A tenant refuses to start when one of these secrets of the control plane has
# a value: WORKOS_API_KEY, WORKOS_CLIENT_SECRET, WORKOS_COOKIE_PASSWORD,
# GATEWAY_SIGNING_KEY, DATABASE_URL, LOGGING_DESTINATION_ENCRYPTION_KEY,
# RENDER_API_KEY, or a STRIPE_* setting. The error names each such setting,
# never a value. docs/design/authentication-design.md#tenant-role-and-signed-access-context
# gives the contract.
# THERMUS_ROLE=control-plane

# TENANT_ORGANIZATION_ID is the one WorkOS organization that a tenant serves:
# 1 to 256 visible ASCII characters. A context of another organization gets 403.
# TENANT_ORGANIZATION_ID=org_example

# GATEWAY_PUBLIC_KEY holds one or more Ed25519 public keys in PEM form
# (-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----), separated by commas. A tenant accepts a context
# of each listed key, so a key rotation lists the old and the new key. A
# private key is refused: only the control plane holds GATEWAY_SIGNING_KEY.
# GATEWAY_PUBLIC_KEY=-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----...-----END PUBLIC KEY-----

# CONTROL_PLANE_INTERNAL_URL is the origin of the control plane on the Render
# private network: http://<slug of the control plane>:8080, the port of the
# server in the image. A tenant posts its audit events to /internal/audit
# there. It is plain http on the private network; https is accepted too.
# A path, a username, a password, a query, or a fragment is refused.
# CONTROL_PLANE_INTERNAL_URL=http://thermus-control-plane:8080

# A tenant also reads WORKOS_AUTHKIT_ISSUER, above, with the same rule: its
# key set checks the Connect token of each request. A tenant gets no other
# WORKOS_* setting.

# Hosted tenant containers. Only the control plane reads these settings.

# MCP_TENANTS_ENABLED is the rollout switch of the tenant containers of the self
# on-boarding plan. It accepts exactly true or false; an empty value or no value
# means false. With false, the control plane reads none of the tenant settings
# below and starts no provisioner, so a wrong tenant setting does not stop the
# start. With true, startup reads and checks each tenant setting below. It also
# needs the private hosted storage of DATABASE_URL below, because the provisioner
# works on the tenant registry. A missing or invalid value stops startup before
# listening. The error names the setting and the rule, but not the value.
# MCP_TENANTS_ENABLED=false

# With true, the provisioner takes one requested row of the tenant registry at a
# time. It creates an image-backed private service on Render for the organization,
# watches its first deploy until it is live, and records the service ID and its
# internal address, http://<slug>:8080. Each new tenant gets THERMUS_ROLE=tenant,
# TENANT_ORGANIZATION_ID, PUBLIC_BASE_URL and WORKOS_AUTHKIT_ISSUER of the control
# plane, GATEWAY_PUBLIC_KEY and CONTROL_PLANE_INTERNAL_URL, and
# OPERATION_CAPACITY=5, the capacity of a free tenant. A tenant gets no secret.

# RENDER_API_KEY is the API key of the Render user of the control plane. Keep it
# secret. Render allows 20 service creates an hour for each Render user, so give
# the control plane its own Render user: then the fleet script, staging, and the
# Render Dashboard do not use its limit.
# RENDER_API_KEY=rnd_example

# RENDER_OWNER_ID is the ID of the Render workspace of the tenants, for example
# tea-example. The tenants are created in the region oregon. The control plane
# must be in the same workspace and region, because only then do the services
# reach each other on the private network.
# RENDER_OWNER_ID=tea-example

# RENDER_REGISTRY_CREDENTIAL_ID is the ID of the Render registry credential that
# reads the hosted image from GHCR, for example rgc-example.
# RENDER_REGISTRY_CREDENTIAL_ID=rgc-example

# TENANT_IMAGE is the hosted image of each new tenant, by digest:
# ghcr.io/<owner>/<name>@sha256:<64 hexadecimal digits>.
# TENANT_IMAGE=ghcr.io/example/thermus-hosted@sha256:<64 hexadecimal digits>

# TENANT_PLAN_FREE is the Render plan of a free tenant, for example 0.5c-512mb.
# TENANT_PLAN_FREE=0.5c-512mb

# TENANT_NAME_PREFIX is the start of the name of each tenant service: the name is
# the prefix and the organization ID in lower case, with each character outside
# a-z, 0-9, and hyphen as a hyphen, so org_01HX... gives thermus-tenant-org-01hx...
# Render shortens a long name into the private hostname at a separator and keeps
# hostnames unique, so the provisioner records the address that Render returns.
# Start with a lowercase letter, and use at most 40 lowercase letters, digits, or
# hyphens. The default is thermus-tenant-, also for an empty value. A staging
# control plane uses thermus-spike-tenant-.
# TENANT_NAME_PREFIX=thermus-tenant-

# With MCP_TENANTS_ENABLED=true, the control plane also reads GATEWAY_PUBLIC_KEY
# and CONTROL_PLANE_INTERNAL_URL, above, with the rules of a tenant, and copies
# them into each new tenant. The control plane never gives a tenant
# GATEWAY_SIGNING_KEY or another secret.

# Hosted private logging settings storage. Missing or invalid settings leave MCP
# available and mark storage unavailable. These values stay on the server.
# Use a full PostgreSQL URI with explicit username and password, host, and
# database. Encode special credential characters. Query parameters and
# fragments are refused, including sslmode and other TLS or host overrides.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://thermus_logging:replace-password@database.example:5432/postgres
# Use canonical base64 for exactly 32 random bytes, separate from the WorkOS
# cookie password. Keep this key secret. Credential encryption follows in S2-01b.
# LOGGING_DESTINATION_ENCRYPTION_KEY=replace-with-base64-32-byte-key
# Optional PEM CA bundle. TLS always checks the certificate and hostname.
# DATABASE_CA_CERT_PATH=/absolute/path/to/database-ca.pem
